{#
	The content part of the message an operator sends a stranded signup (XIV-108,
	§8.14), drawn inside `@XiviCore/email/base.html.twig` like every other mail
	this application sends (§5.13). This file is only the middle; `SignupMailer`
	renders it to a string and hands it in as `content`.

	Inline styles for the frame's reason: Gmail and Outlook both drop a <style>
	block often enough that relying on one is relying on luck.

	**The same sentences as the text part, in the same order, and no others.** The
	text part is what an operator previews before pressing send, so a paragraph
	that appeared only here would be a paragraph nobody approved, which is the
	one thing this feature is built to prevent. Adding a sentence means adding it
	to both files, and the catalogue key is what keeps them saying the same thing.

	**Nothing here is a link or a button**, unlike the confirmation mail. See the
	text part for why: there is nothing to click, and a link in this particular
	message would be the shape of the mail its reader ought to distrust.
#}
<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.stalled.greeting'|trans({'%company%': company}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.stalled.body'|trans({'%slug%': slug}) }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.stalled.hand'|trans }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">{{ 'signup.stalled.next'|trans }}</p>

<p style="margin: 0; color: #6c757d; font-size: 13px;">{{ 'signup.stalled.no_action'|trans }}</p>
